## 6. Adding `torch.compile` Support (FakeTensor / Meta Kernel)

To add `torch.compile` support, add a **FakeTensor kernel** (a.k.a. "meta kernel" or "abstract impl"). FakeTensors have metadata (shape, dtype, device) but no data; the kernel computes output metadata from input metadata and should return dummy Tensors with correct metadata (shape/strides/dtype/device).

Recommended via Python `torch.library.register_fake` (possible from C++ too — see the Custom Operators Manual).

```python
# Important: the C++ custom operator definitions should be loaded first
# before calling ``torch.library`` APIs that add registrations for the
# C++ custom operator(s). The following import loads our
# C++ custom operator definitions.
# Note that if you are striving for Python agnosticism, you should use
# the ``load_library(...)`` API call instead. See the next section for
# more details.
from . import _C

@torch.library.register_fake("extension_cpp::mymuladd")
def _(a, b, c):
    torch._check(a.shape == b.shape)
    torch._check(a.dtype == torch.float)
    torch._check(b.dtype == torch.float)
    torch._check(a.device == b.device)
    return torch.empty_like(a)
```

---

## 7. Hybrid Python/C++ Registration (Loading Order)

The tutorial defines a custom operator in C++, adds CPU/CUDA implementations in C++, and adds FakeTensor kernels and backward formulas in Python. **Registration/import order matters** (wrong order → error). Must first load the C++ library holding the operator definition, then call `torch.library` registration APIs. Three ways:

### Way 1 — `PYBIND11_MODULE` dummy module (breaks CPython agnosticism)

```cpp
// in, say, not_agnostic/csrc/extension_BAD.cpp
#include <pybind11/pybind11.h>

PYBIND11_MODULE("_C", m) {}
```
```python
# in, say, extension/__init__.py
from . import _C
```

> "...you will notice that this does not compile with `Py_LIMITED_API`, because `pybind11` does not promise to only use the stable limited CPython API! With the below code, you sadly cannot build a CPython agnostic wheel for your extension!"

### Way 2 — Stable API dummy `_C` module (recommended; compiles with `-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000`)

> See [extension_cpp/__init__.py](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/blob/38ec45e/extension_cpp/__init__.py) and [extension_cpp/csrc/muladd.cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/blob/38ec45e/extension_cpp/csrc/muladd.cpp).

```cpp
#include <Python.h>

extern "C" {
  /* Creates a dummy empty _C module that can be imported from Python.
    The import from Python will load the .so consisting of this file
    in this extension, so that the TORCH_LIBRARY static initializers
    below are run. */
  PyObject* PyInit__C(void)
  {
      static struct PyModuleDef module_def = {
          PyModuleDef_HEAD_INIT,
          "_C",   /* name of module */
          NULL,   /* module documentation, may be NULL */
          -1,     /* size of per-interpreter state of the module,
                    or -1 if the module keeps state in global variables. */
          NULL,   /* methods */
      };
      return PyModule_Create(&module_def);
  }
}
```
```python
from . import _C
```

### Way 3 — `torch.ops.load_library` (avoids `Python.h` entirely)

> "Note that, with this method, there is no `_C` Python module created for the extension so you cannot call `import _C` from Python."

```python
import torch
from pathlib import Path

so_files = list(Path(__file__).parent.glob("_C*.so"))
assert (
    len(so_files) == 1
), f"Expected one _C*.so file, found {len(so_files)}"
torch.ops.load_library(so_files[0])

from . import ops
```

